  {
   "id": "aczqzvjde9IJ",
   "from": "Jean-Olivier Irisson",
   "author": "d31339b95f6e694c",
   "date": "2012-07-31T20:43:10Z",
   "subject": "Re: broken geom_text after update to 0.91",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "On 2012-Jul-31, at 22:37 , Giovanni Azua &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">brav...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; I had in ggplot2 the following plot being generated OK where text labels &quot;Scalar peak performance&quot; and &quot;Vector peak performance&quot; looked fine. After updating to 0.91 I get the text labels looking screwed up like this:\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "It&#39;s because the text is repeated as many times as there are lines in your data frame. Try putting the data=df in the geoms that need it and not in the gloabl ggplot() call. If that does not work, you&#39;ll want to create a custom data.frame with just the required data.\r<br>\r<br>But overall, I think this text does not look like data, more like a custom annotation. So for this you should prefer annotate() with geom=&quot;text&quot;. You would expect the horizontal lines to be the same (i.e. annotate with geom=&quot;hline&quot;) but for some reason I never managed to get this working...\r<br>\r<br>Jean-Olivier Irisson\r<br>---\r<br>Observatoire Océanologique\r<br>Station Zoologique, B.P. 28, Chemin du Lazaret\r<br>06230 Villefranche-sur-Mer\r<br>Tel: <a href=\"tel:+33%204%2093%2076%2038%2004\" value=\"+33493763804\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\">+33 04 93 76 38 04</a>\r<br>Mob: <a href=\"tel:+33%206%2021%2005%2019%2090\" value=\"+33621051990\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\">+33 06 21 05 19 90</a>\r<br><a href=\"http://jo.irisson.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://jo.irisson.com/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792375036544000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1VbJ_9o2sW9Ut4BuPMsxDr\">http://jo.irisson.com/</a>\r<br>Send me large files at: <a href=\"http://jo.irisson.com/upload/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://jo.irisson.com/upload/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792375036544000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2gogfZWK_0SwDMnp5Khcgl\">http://jo.irisson.com/upload/</a>\r<br>\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "sbSUoc53tOsJ",
   "from": "Winston Chang",
   "author": "d34d025a513c07fc",
   "date": "2012-07-31T20:56:19Z",
   "subject": "Re: broken geom_text after update to 0.91",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "On Tue, Jul 31, 2012 at 3:43 PM, Jean-Olivier Irisson\r<br>&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">iri...@normalesup.org</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; On 2012-Jul-31, at 22:37 , Giovanni Azua &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">brav...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt;&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; I had in ggplot2 the following plot being generated OK where text labels &quot;Scalar peak performance&quot; and &quot;Vector peak performance&quot; looked fine. After updating to 0.91 I get the text labels looking screwed up like this:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; It&#39;s because the text is repeated as many times as there are lines in your data frame. Try putting the data=df in the geoms that need it and not in the gloabl ggplot() call. If that does not work, you&#39;ll want to create a custom data.frame with just the required data.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; But overall, I think this text does not look like data, more like a custom annotation. So for this you should prefer annotate() with geom=&quot;text&quot;. You would expect the horizontal lines to be the same (i.e. annotate with geom=&quot;hline&quot;) but for some reason I never managed to get this working...\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "Jean-Olivier is right - you want something like:\r<br>  annotate(geom=&quot;text&quot;, x=5, y=5, label=&quot;text label&quot;)\r<br>\r<br>In general when you want to add a single geom, you can use annotate()\r<br>and specify the geom.\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>The only exception is that annotate() doesn&#39;t work with hline, vline,\r<br>and abline. There is some long-standing code (probably from before\r<br>annotate() existed) in those geoms that handles the special case where\r<br>the intercept is set to a specific value. When you do that, it will\r<br>only plot one line, instead of one for each row in the data frame.\r<br>Unfortunately, this code also makes it not work properly with\r<br>annotate().\r<br>\r<br>There&#39;s a patch waiting to be merged that will change the behavior to\r<br>be like other geoms. The new behavior can break existing user code, so\r<br>it&#39;s waiting for the next major release to be merged.\r<br>\r<br>-Winston\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "_bgMFRfUOUgJ",
   "from": "Giovanni Azua",
   "author": "51af56f8dba6ef19",
   "date": "2012-08-01T08:17:14Z",
   "subject": "Re: broken geom_text after update to 0.91",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><div><br></div><div>Fantastic! thank you so much! &#39;annotate&#39; worked perfectly.</div><div><br></div><div>On the side note, I had to keep the hacky code e.g. <span style=\"font-family:Monaco;font-size:14px\">guide_grid_orig</span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco;font-size:14px\"><span style=\"color:#171799\"> &lt;- </span></span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco;font-size:14px\">ggplot2</span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco;font-size:14px\"><span style=\"color:#171799\">:::</span></span><span style=\"font-family:Monaco;font-size:14px\">guide_grid </span>to be able to display the desired grid style. Is this fixed in 0.91 or is there an alternative way to configure it?</div><div><br></div><div>Best regards,</div><div>Giovanni </div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><br><div><div>On Jul 31, 2012, at 10:56 PM, Winston Chang wrote:</div><br><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div>On Tue, Jul 31, 2012 at 3:43 PM, Jean-Olivier Irisson<br>&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">iri...@normalesup.org</a>&gt; wrote:<br><blockquote type=\"cite\">On 2012-Jul-31, at 22:37 , Giovanni Azua &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">brav...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></blockquote><blockquote type=\"cite\"><blockquote type=\"cite\"><br></blockquote></blockquote><blockquote type=\"cite\"><blockquote type=\"cite\">I had in ggplot2 the following plot being generated OK where text labels &quot;Scalar peak performance&quot; and &quot;Vector peak performance&quot; looked fine. After updating to 0.91 I get the text labels looking screwed up like this:<br></blockquote></blockquote><blockquote type=\"cite\"><br></blockquote><blockquote type=\"cite\">It&#39;s because the text is repeated as many times as there are lines in your data frame. Try putting the data=df in the geoms that need it and not in the gloabl ggplot() call. If that does not work, you&#39;ll want to create a custom data.frame with just the required data.<br></blockquote><blockquote type=\"cite\"><br></blockquote><blockquote type=\"cite\">But overall, I think this text does not look like data, more like a custom annotation. So for this you should prefer annotate() with geom=&quot;text&quot;. You would expect the horizontal lines to be the same (i.e. annotate with geom=&quot;hline&quot;) but for some reason I never managed to get this working...<br></blockquote><br><br>Jean-Olivier is right - you want something like:<br>  annotate(geom=&quot;text&quot;, x=5, y=5, label=&quot;text label&quot;)<br><br>In general when you want to add a single geom, you can use annotate()<br>and specify the geom.<br><br><br>The only exception is that annotate() doesn&#39;t work with hline, vline,<br>and abline. There is some long-standing code (probably from before<br>annotate() existed) in those geoms that handles the special case where<br>the intercept is set to a specific value. When you do that, it will<br>only plot one line, instead of one for each row in the data frame.<br>Unfortunately, this code also makes it not work properly with<br>annotate().<br><br>There&#39;s a patch waiting to be merged that will change the behavior to<br>be like other geoms. The new behavior can break existing user code, so<br>it&#39;s waiting for the next major release to be merged.<br><br>-Winston<br></div></blockquote></div><br></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
